<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuevo León</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuevo León is a region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a>. It grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of León, a frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> granted to Luis de Carvajal in 1579, and supplied settlers for neighbouring Nuevo Santander. After independence it became a state of Mexico, and its federalists joined the short-lived Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> in 1840.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon.jpg" alt="Nuevo León" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathaniel C. Sheetz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerro_de_la_Silla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nuevo León, officially the Free and Sovereign State of Nuevo León, lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, and has an extremely narrow international border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. It covers 64,156 square kilometres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a>, the capital, is its largest city. Luis Carvajal y de la Cueva named the region the New Kingdom of León after the Kingdom of León, noting similarities in their mountainous terrain, and after independence the name was shortened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before European colonisation there was no established state. Historians estimate that about 250 nomadic groups lived in the territory, which the Spaniards called chichimecas. Four main groups can be identified: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuiltecan">Coahuiltecans</a> to the west, the Alazapas to the north, the Borrados to the east and the Guachichiles to the south. A colonisation attempt under Alberto del Canto in 1577 founded Santa Lucía on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> but failed because of raids by the indigenous people, whom the colonisers labelled Indios Bárbaros.</p><p>Luis Carvajal y de la Cueva, who had been with del Canto, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and negotiated with Philip II to establish a new territory in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. He founded the New Kingdom of León in 1582, with its capital San Luis Rey de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> on the site of Santa Lucía. Colonisation failed again in 1588, when Carvajal was reported to the Inquisition as a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>, and the region remained mostly uninhabited by the Spaniards. Permanent settlement did not occur until 1596.</p><p>News of the Cry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a> of 16 September 1810 reached the region only on 29 September. Governor Manuel de Santa María first prepared to defend it, but after the insurgent General José Mariano Jiménez advanced he changed sides and proclaimed the insurgency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> on 17 January 1811. Nuevo León became a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1824. Las <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a> of 1835 replaced the state with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, and in reply a secessionist movement set up the unrecognised Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> in 1840, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. It ended after defeat at the Battle of Saltillo, and statehood returned by August 1846 with the restoration of the 1824 constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfiriato">Porfiriato</a>, Bernardo Reyes was governor from 1885 to 1887 and from 1889 to 1909. In 1892 he negotiated a land swap with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> that gave Nuevo León access to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. His policies industrialised the state: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a> brewery was founded in 1900, the Monterrey Foundry in 1904, Cementos Hidalgo, later Cemex, in 1905 and Vidrios y Cristales de Monterrey in 1909. Industrialisation continued through the 20th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuevo León” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The commandant general held military, political and judicial authority over Nueva Vizcaya, Sonora and Sinaloa, the Californias, New Mexico, Coahuila and Texas, and at times <b>Nuevo León</b> and Nuevo Santander.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1824, after independence, the province became the state of <b>Nuevo León</b> in the First Mexican Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-kingdom-of-leon">New Kingdom of León</a></li><li><blockquote>Escandón governed as colonist and military commander, granting land to settler families from <b>Nuevo León</b> and Querétaro who raised cattle, sheep and horses on large ranchos.</blockquote><a href="/state/nuevo-santander">Nuevo Santander</a></li><li><blockquote>Opposition to the Centralist Republic of Mexico ran deep in the northern frontier states of Tamaulipas, <b>Nuevo León</b> and Coahuila.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-rio-grande">Republic of the Rio Grande</a></li></ul></section>
